    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nOwners may now include an optional 4-digit `postcode`, validated against the city's region when present, and stored/returned when given \u2014 while staying backward-compatible for owners created without one.\n\n## Where each piece of logic lives\n\n**Format (\"4 digits\")** \u2014 added `postcode` to the `OwnerFields` schema in `openapi.yml` with `pattern: '^[0-9]{4}$'`. This regenerates `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` with Bean Validation, so a malformed postcode (e.g. `\"12\"`) is rejected with 400 the same idiomatic way as `telephone` and the name fields. Because it's not in `required`, absence is accepted.\n\n**Range validation (region table)** \u2014 new `PostcodeValidator` (in `rest/validation`) owns the fixed region\u2192range table (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) and reuses the existing `CityRegion.regionFor(city)` for the city\u2192region lookup. `isValidForCity` returns `true` for absent postcodes and for cities with no known region (the permissive default), and otherwise checks the inclusive range.\n\n**Error surface** \u2014 new `InvalidPostcodeException` plus a handler in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` returning 400 with `errors: [\"postcode\"]`, mirroring the existing `InvalidTelephoneException` pattern.\n\n**Wiring** \u2014 `addOwner` in `OwnerRestControllerV1` calls the validator right after mapping and throws when out of range.\n\n**Persistence** \u2014 added a `postcode` field/column to the `Owner` entity and to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres). MapStruct maps the field automatically by name, so no mapper changes were needed.\n\n## Tests\n\n`AcceptanceTest` (the only test class) passes \u2014 3/3, `BUILD SUCCESS`: valid postcode stored and returned, owner without a postcode accepted, and a malformed postcode rejected with 400.",
